  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the probability of a favorable (positive) outcome, treating all positive outcomes similarly. The individual’s reasoning highlights that B guarantees a win (i.e., a favorable outcome) while A does not — this directly corresponds to B having a higher probability (100% vs 50%) of a favorable outcome. Although the individual refers to expected value ('rechnerisch auf Null'), their final justification centers on the certainty of a gain, which aligns with the core logic of the decision reason. The individual does not explicitly state that outcome differences are negligible, but their preference for B based solely on guaranteed gain (despite lower maximum payoff) implies that they treat the gain in B as sufficiently favorable, even if smaller. Thus, the information considered (certainty of positive outcome) and the justification (choosing the sure gain) are consistent with the decision reason. Minor deviation exists in not explicitly stating outcome equivalence, but behaviorally and cognitively, the reasoning aligns closely.",
